\maketitle

% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Iris biometric systems are vulnerable to presentation attacks including
printed irises, electronic displays, contact lenses, artificially
generated images, and post-mortem samples. Existing supervised detectors
require labeled attack data during training and fail to generalize to
unseen attack types. We propose an unsupervised iris Presentation Attack
Detection (PAD) framework based on the Latent Brownian Bridge Diffusion
Model (LBBDM-f4), trained exclusively on bona fide iris images.
At inference, attack images produce higher perceptual reconstruction
error because the model has learned only genuine iris texture.
We evaluate on 47{,}434 test images spanning 8 attack types and
achieve \textbf{30.51\% ACER} and \textbf{AUC = 0.748} with zero attack
supervision, outperforming all 10 supervised CNN baselines tested on the
same dataset (best CNN ACER = 38.39\%) and the AnoDDPM-based anomaly
detector (best ACER = 44.00\%). Post-mortem and artifact attacks are
detected at 100\% and 99.27\% rates respectively without any attack
training examples. Code and models will be made publicly available.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Iris PAD, presentation attack detection, diffusion models,
Brownian bridge, anomaly detection, unsupervised, biometrics
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Iris recognition is among the most accurate biometric modalities,
yet it remains susceptible to presentation attacks (PAs).
An attacker may present a printed iris photograph, a replay attack
via an electronic display, a patterned contact lens, a
synthetically generated image, or even a post-mortem sample to
spoof a live capture system.

Existing PAD approaches are predominantly \emph{supervised}:
they train on known attack types and learn discriminative features
between bona fide and attack classes~\cite{livdet2025}.
This creates a fundamental generalization problem --- a model trained
on printed and contact-lens attacks will struggle against novel
attack types encountered in deployment.

We address this with an \textbf{unsupervised anomaly detection}
paradigm: train only on bona fide iris images, and flag any image
that reconstructs poorly as a potential attack.
Specifically, we adapt the Latent Brownian Bridge Diffusion Model
(LBBDM-f4)~\cite{bbdm} to learn the distribution of genuine iris
texture. At inference, the model attempts to reconstruct a test image
from a corrupted version; attacks produce higher LPIPS perceptual
error because their textures lie outside the learned manifold.

\textbf{Contributions:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item First application of Brownian Bridge Diffusion Models to
        iris PAD.
  \item Fully unsupervised pipeline: zero attack examples during
        training.
  \item Comprehensive evaluation across 8 attack types with
        47{,}434 test images, including an ablation against
        AnoDDPM~\cite{anoddpm} and 10 supervised CNN baselines.
  \item ACER = 30.51\%, AUC = 0.748 --- surpassing all supervised
        baselines on the same dataset.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Supervised Iris PAD}
DenseNet~\cite{densenet}, MobileNet, EfficientNet, and SENet-based
classifiers have been applied to iris PAD with strong performance
when attack types are known at training time.
LivDet-Iris 2025~\cite{livdet2025} shows the current state of the
art: supervised systems achieve AUROC up to 0.906.
However, all top entries are supervised, and performance degrades
significantly on held-out attack types.

\subsection{Anomaly Detection for PAD}
Unsupervised anomaly detection trains on bona fide data only.
Auto-encoder reconstruction error has been applied to face PAD,
but diffusion-based reconstruction is more expressive.
AnoDDPM~\cite{anoddpm} uses partial noising-and-denoising with a
standard DDPM to detect anomalies via MSE reconstruction error.
We extend this idea using Brownian Bridge diffusion, which provides
stronger structural constraints and richer perceptual scoring.

\subsection{Diffusion Models for Biometrics}
DDPM~\cite{ddpm} and its latent variant LDM~\cite{ldm} have been
applied to face generation and attribute editing.
BBDM~\cite{bbdm} extends diffusion to image translation via
Brownian Bridge stochastic processes, achieving strong results on
paired image-to-image tasks. We repurpose it as an anomaly detector
by training it on corrupted-to-clean bona fide pairs.

% ============================================================
\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Brownian Bridge Diffusion Model}

A Brownian Bridge process connects two fixed endpoints
$\mathbf{x}_0$ (clean image) and $\mathbf{x}_T$ (noisy/corrupted
image) with a stochastic path. Unlike standard DDPM where
$\mathbf{x}_T \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{I})$, the bridge endpoint
is the corrupted input $\mathbf{y}$:
\begin{equation}
  q(\mathbf{z}_t \mid \mathbf{z}_0, \mathbf{y}) =
  \mathcal{N}\!\left(
    \mathbf{z}_t;\,
    \left(1 - \frac{t}{T}\right)\mathbf{z}_0 + \frac{t}{T}\mathbf{y},\,
    \frac{t(T-t)}{T}\mathbf{I}
  \right)
\end{equation}
The reverse denoising process reconstructs the clean latent
$\mathbf{z}_0$ from $\mathbf{z}_T \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{y},
\sigma^2\mathbf{I})$, guided by $\mathbf{y}$ at every step.
This conditioning anchors reconstruction to the input, making
the perceptual error a precise measure of how anomalous the input is.

\subsection{Latent Space}
We use the VQ-f4 encoder from LDM~\cite{ldm} to project
$256{\times}256$ images to $64{\times}64{\times}3$ latents.
The bridge and UNet operate in this compressed space, preserving
fine iris texture at manageable GPU memory cost.

\subsection{Training Pipeline (Bona Fide Only)}

\textbf{Preprocessing.}
Bona fide iris images are segmented using Hough circle transform,
resized to $256{\times}256$, and saved as RGB PNGs.

\textbf{Corruption.}
Each bona fide image is independently corrupted to create a paired
$(A, B)$ training sample:
$A$ = Gaussian noise ($\sigma{=}0.1$) + Gaussian blur ($k{=}3$)
+ $2{\times}$ resolution downscale-upscale;
$B$ = clean original.

\textbf{Training.}
LBBDM-f4 is trained for 200 epochs on these pairs using Adam
($\text{lr}{=}10^{-4}$), batch size 16, on an NVIDIA RTX A5000
(25.4 GB VRAM). The model learns the mapping from corrupted to
clean bona fide iris texture.

\subsection{Inference and Scoring}

At test time a single image $\mathbf{x}$ (bona fide or attack) is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Corrupted with the same pipeline to produce $\mathbf{y}$.
  \item Encoded: $\mathbf{z}_0 = \text{Enc}(\mathbf{x})$,
        $\mathbf{z}_y = \text{Enc}(\mathbf{y})$.
  \item Diffused to $\mathbf{z}_T \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}_y, \sigma^2)$.
  \item Denoised over 200 linear skip steps to $\hat{\mathbf{z}}_0$.
  \item Decoded: $\hat{\mathbf{x}} = \text{Dec}(\hat{\mathbf{z}}_0)$.
\end{enumerate}
Five reconstructions are produced per image: one primary pass plus
four trajectory samples at $t \in \{100, 250, 500, 750\}$, yielding
five PAD score columns. We evaluate:

\begin{align}
s_{\text{MSE}}  &= \|\mathbf{x} - \hat{\mathbf{x}}\|_2^2 \\
s_{\text{LPIPS}}&= \text{LPIPS}(\mathbf{x},\, \hat{\mathbf{x}}) \\
s_{\text{traj}} &= \text{Var}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}^{(100)},
                    \hat{\mathbf{x}}^{(250)},
                    \hat{\mathbf{x}}^{(500)},
                    \hat{\mathbf{x}}^{(750)})
\end{align}

Image $\mathbf{x}$ is classified as an attack if
$s_{\text{LPIPS}} > \tau$, where $\tau$ is chosen to minimise ACER
on the validation set.

% ============================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Dataset}
We use the IIT Mandi iris PAD dataset comprising 125{,}854 images
spanning 8 attack types plus bona fide.
Table~\ref{tab:dataset} summarises the splits used.

\begin{table}[!h]
\centering
\caption{Dataset split summary.}
\label{tab:dataset}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
\rowcolor{headercol}
\textbf{Split} & \textbf{Bonafide} & \textbf{Attack} & \textbf{Total} \\
\midrule
Train (BBDM) & 14{,}028 pairs & 0 & 14{,}028 \\
Validation    & 5{,}276 & 16{,}104 & 21{,}380 \\
Test          & 12{,}926 & 34{,}508 & 47{,}434 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Attack types (test counts):}} \\
\multicolumn{4}{l}{Printed (7{,}497), CL (6{,}304), E-display (5{,}546),} \\
\multicolumn{4}{l}{Print+E-display (5{,}180), Generated (4{,}978),} \\
\multicolumn{4}{l}{PostMortem (3{,}293), Artifact (1{,}643), Fake+Addon (67)} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{APCER}: Attack Presentation Classification Error Rate
        (fraction of attacks wrongly accepted as bona fide).
  \item \textbf{BPCER}: Bona fide Presentation Classification Error Rate
        (fraction of bona fide wrongly rejected as attacks).
  \item \textbf{ACER} = (APCER + BPCER) / 2 --- primary metric.
  \item \textbf{EER}: Equal Error Rate (threshold-free estimate).
  \item \textbf{AUC}: Area Under the ROC Curve (threshold-free ranking).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Baselines}
\textbf{CNN Classifiers (supervised).}
Ten CNN models are trained in a fully supervised setting on the same
training split, including DenseNet-121~\cite{densenet},
EfficientNetV2-S, MobileNetV2, MobileNetV3-Large, and SENet in
both full fine-tune and last-block-only variants.

\textbf{AnoDDPM (unsupervised).}
We implement four configurations of the Wyatt et al.~\cite{anoddpm}
framework using the same VQGAN backbone:
(C1) Gaussian noise, $t^*{=}1000$, 25 stochastic steps;
(C2) Gaussian, $t^*{=}500$, 25 steps;
(C3) Simplex noise~\cite{anoddpm}, $t^*{=}500$, 25 steps;
(C4) Simplex + DDIM, $t^*{=}500$, 50 steps.

% ============================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Overall PAD Performance}

Table~\ref{tab:overall} presents the primary comparison.
BBDM achieves the best ACER (30.51\%) and AUC (0.748) among all
methods. The best supervised CNN (DenseNet-121 LastBlock) achieves
38.39\% ACER despite having access to labeled attack data during
training. All AnoDDPM variants perform near random (AUC $\approx$ 0.49)
because the MSE score gap between bona fide and attack collapses to
near zero across the full 8-attack-type distribution.

