\subsection{vs.\ Supervised Baselines (Same Dataset)}

Our 44 supervised baselines (10 architectures $\times$ multiple
training strategies) were tested in an open-set protocol:
trained on 7/8 attack types, tested on the held-out type.
BBDM is trained on 0/8 attack types.
Despite this extreme disadvantage, BBDM achieves lower ACER
(30.51\%) than all supervised architectures evaluated in the
closed-set protocol (best: 38.39\%).


% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Why BBDM Outperforms AnoDDPM}

Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison} summarises the key architectural
differences. The fundamental advantage of BBDM lies in its
conditioning: the model receives the corrupted input $\mathbf{y}$
at every denoising step, anchoring the reconstruction to the
specific input image. AnoDDPM is unconditional --- the denoising
path is driven entirely by the learned prior, which cannot adapt
to fine iris texture variations between attack types.

\begin{table}[!h]
\centering
\caption{Architectural comparison: BBDM vs.\ AnoDDPM.}
\label{tab:method_comparison}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.15}
\begin{adjustbox}{max width=\columnwidth}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\rowcolor{headercol}
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{BBDM (Ours)} & \textbf{AnoDDPM} \\
\midrule
Input at inference  & Image + corrupted ref.\ & Single image only \\
Conditioning        & Yes (every step)          & No (unconditional) \\
Forward process     & Brownian Bridge $A\!\to\!B$ & Standard DDPM \\
Noise type          & Gaussian (bridge)         & Gaussian / Simplex \\
Inference steps     & 200 (linear skip)         & 25--50 \\
Passes per image    & 5                         & 1 \\
Score signals       & MSE, LPIPS, Recon,        & MSE, LPIPS, Recon \\
                    & Trajectory, Combined      &  \\
Trajectory score    & Yes (4 bridge points)     & No \\
Best score          & LPIPS                     & MSE \\
Score gap (bf/atk)  & $+0.105$ LPIPS (+57\%)    & $+0.002$ MSE ($\approx 0$\%) \\
\midrule
Val ACER            & \textbf{22.94\%}          & 43.05\% \\
Test ACER           & \textbf{30.51\%}          & 44.00\% \\
Test AUC            & \textbf{0.748}            & 0.488 \\
UNet fwd.\ passes   & 1000 / image              & 25--50 / image \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{adjustbox}
\end{table}

The score gap quantifies discriminability: BBDM achieves
$+57\%$ separation in LPIPS between bona fide and attack,
while AnoDDPM achieves near-zero MSE gap ($+0.002$) --- making
its threshold placement essentially arbitrary.

\subsection{Attack-Type Difficulty Analysis}

Physical and material attacks (PostMortem, Artifact) are detected
near-perfectly because their textures are maximally different from
genuine iris: post-mortem samples exhibit desiccation artifacts,
and artificial iris patterns are not naturalistic.
Display-based attacks (E-display, Contact Lens) share frequency
and texture characteristics with genuine irises, making them
inherently harder for reconstruction-based detectors.

\subsection{APCER--BPCER Trade-off}

BBDM operates at higher BPCER (42.22\%) than supervised CNNs
because its threshold is calibrated on a validation set with a
72.7\% attack rate, biasing detection sensitivity.
Supervised CNNs operate at very low BPCER ($<$10\%) because they
learn discriminative features rather than reconstruction fidelity.
In operational deployment, the threshold can be adjusted to meet
application-specific BPCER constraints without retraining.

\subsection{Label Quality Impact (AnoDDPM Evaluation)}

We identified and corrected a critical labeling bug in the AnoDDPM
evaluation pipeline: the original labels CSV assigned
\texttt{unknown} to 32{,}164 of 34{,}508 test attacks (67.8\%)
and 15{,}200 of 16{,}104 validation attacks (71.1\%).
Only Artifact and part of CL were correctly labeled.
After correction (labels extracted from filenames), AnoDDPM ACER
increases from $\sim$31\% to 44--48\% and AUC collapses from
$\sim$0.76 to $\sim$0.49, revealing that the model provides
essentially no discriminative signal across the full attack
distribution.


