% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented an unsupervised iris PAD framework based on the Latent
Brownian Bridge Diffusion Model (LBBDM-f4). Trained exclusively on
bona fide iris images, the model achieves \textbf{30.51\% ACER}
and \textbf{AUC = 0.748} on a challenging test set of 47{,}434
images spanning 8 attack types --- outperforming all 10 supervised
CNN baselines (best: 38.39\%) and the AnoDDPM anomaly detector
(best: 44.00\%).

Key findings:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Conditioning matters}: BBDM's bridge conditioning
        provides a 57\% LPIPS score gap between bona fide and attacks.
        AnoDDPM without conditioning achieves near-zero MSE gap.
  \item \textbf{LPIPS $>$ MSE}: Perceptual distance outperforms
        pixel-level MSE for detecting fine iris texture anomalies.
  \item \textbf{100 steps suffices}: Reducing from 200 to 100
        denoising steps cuts inference time by 48\% with negligible
        ACER increase (26.71\% $\to$ 26.66\%).
  \item \textbf{Physical attacks are easiest}: PostMortem (100\%)
        and Artifact (99.27\%) are detected near-perfectly without
        any attack training data.
  \item \textbf{Unsupervised $\approx$ supervised}: Our method
        achieves competitive results with no attack supervision,
        demonstrating the viability of diffusion-based anomaly
        detection for open-set iris PAD.
\end{enumerate}

Future work will explore self-reconstruction pairing,
resolution-adaptive scoring, and multi-scale trajectory analysis
to further close the gap with supervised methods on display-based
attacks.

